>
  <h2>TL;DR: define the handoff before the homepage</h2>
  <p>
    An established practice should expect a website partner to define what will be built,
    what will migrate, what the practice must approve, who controls each account, how booking
    will be tested, and what evidence will count as launch-ready. Design matters, but a polished
    homepage does not replace a URL inventory, content approvals, accessible form checks,
    analytics continuity, or written ownership and support terms.
  </p>
  <p>
    ChiroCandy offers custom chiropractic websites with content and technical foundations.
    Because every existing site and agreement is different, the proposal for your practice
    should identify the exact pages, integrations, migration work, launch checks, and ongoing
    responsibilities. A website can support discovery and make the next step clearer; it cannot
    guarantee rankings, appointments, patients, or revenue.
  </p>

  <h2>What should a chiropractic website redesign include?</h2>
  <p>
    Use the scope below as a proposal checklist. Not every practice needs every item, but an
    omitted workstream should be a deliberate decision—not a surprise discovered during launch.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Website redesign scope and acceptance evidence">
    <table>
      <caption>Website scope and acceptance framework</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Workstream</th>
          <th scope="col">Define before work starts</th>
          <th scope="col">Evidence before acceptance</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Strategy and pages</th>
          <td>Audiences, locations, services, primary calls to action, page list, and decision owner</td>
          <td>Approved sitemap and a named reviewer for each sensitive claim</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Design and content</th>
          <td>Custom versus reused components, copy responsibilities, photography, revision rounds, and approvals</td>
          <td>Approved representative pages using publishable text and licensed media</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Booking and contact</th>
          <td>Scheduler, appointment types, locations, phone routing, forms, notifications, and test method</td>
          <td>Dated mobile, desktop, and keyboard checks with the correct destination and recipient</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Migration and search</th>
          <td>Current URL inventory, retained pages, changed URLs, redirects, metadata, canonicals, sitemap, and crawl controls</td>
          <td>URL-to-URL redirect map plus checks for status, canonical, internal links, and index directives</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Measurement</th>
          <td>Analytics and Search Console access, consent requirements, events, baseline date, and reporting owner</td>
          <td>Practice-controlled access and observed receipt of approved test events</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Launch and support</th>
          <td>Launch owner, DNS responsibility, rollback path, warranty window, maintenance, requests, response expectations, and fees</td>
          <td>Signed acceptance list, access handoff, unresolved-issue log, and support instructions</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What ChiroCandy publicly offers—and what your agreement should settle</h2>
  <p>
    ChiroCandy's public service and <a href="/pricing/">pricing information</a> describe a custom,
    conversion-focused website with clear content and technical foundations. The current pricing
    page also states that a custom website requires a 12-month website agreement. Those public
    descriptions are a starting point, not a substitute for the proposal and agreement covering
    your practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before signing, ask the ChiroCandy team to put the page list, content responsibilities,
    integrations, migration work, hosting, support, revision process, access, ownership,
    cancellation, and transition terms in writing. If an item is not included, identify who will
    perform it and when. This protects both the practice and the delivery team from relying on a
    sales conversation as the project specification.
  </p>

  <h2>Protect the existing site during migration</h2>
  <p>
    A redesign is also a migration. Start with a crawl or export of the current URLs and classify
    each useful page as retain, revise, merge, redirect, or remove. When a URL changes, map it to
    the most relevant new destination rather than sending unrelated pages to the homepage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google's current <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/site-move-with-url-changes">site-move guidance</a>
    recommends preparing a URL mapping, implementing redirects, updating internal links and
    canonicals, removing temporary <code>noindex</code> rules at launch, submitting the current
    sitemap, and monitoring the move. Google also warns that visibility can fluctuate while a
    changed site is recrawled and reindexed. Treat a migration plan as risk control, not a ranking
    promise.
  </p>
  <p>
    Preserve analytics and Search Console access before changing platforms or DNS. Record the
    launch date and major URL or tracking changes so later reports do not compare unlike periods
    without explanation. If the existing site earns calls, forms, or organic visits, test those
    paths before decommissioning it.
  </p>

  <h2>Test the patient path, not just the page design</h2>
  <p>
    Pick representative paths for a new visitor: a service page, a location page if applicable,
    the team or provider information, and the next step. On supported phone and desktop sizes,
    verify that navigation, click-to-call links, forms, and the booking experience remain readable
    and usable without horizontal overflow or hidden controls. Use the keyboard to reach and operate
    the navigation, form fields, booking controls, and any dialog or menu in the path.
  </p>
  <p>
    For forms, the W3C's <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/forms/">forms guidance</a>
    recommends identifying controls with labels, giving instructions where needed, validating
    input, and providing clear feedback that helps a person correct an error. These checks are a
    practical first review, not a claim that a short checklist proves full accessibility or legal
    compliance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test booking with an approved non-patient record. Confirm the location, appointment type,
    timezone, confirmation message, staff notification, and follow-up destination. A successful
    button click is not the same as a correctly routed appointment request.
  </p>

  <h2>Decide ownership, access, and portability in writing</h2>
  <p>
    The practice should know who controls the domain registration and billing, DNS, hosting,
    content management system, analytics, Search Console, scheduler, call tracking, licensed
    media, approved copy, and design or source files. Administrator access is different from
    ownership, and an export may not include every component needed to operate the site elsewhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ask what can be exported, in which format, which third-party licenses can transfer, what
    assistance is available during a transition, and what happens to redirects, forms, tracking,
    and integrations after cancellation. ChiroCandy's public pricing page says ownership and
    cancellation details are covered in the agreement, so use the written agreement—not an
    assumption—to answer these questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a deeper handoff review, use the <a href="/blog/chiropractic-domain-website-ownership/">domain,
    website, and account ownership checklist</a> and the <a href="/blog/switching-chiropractic-website-providers/">website-provider
    switching checklist</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Use a pass, revise, or blocked launch decision</h2>
  <p>
    A launch review should end with one of three plain decisions. <strong>Pass</strong> means the
    defined acceptance checks succeeded and the practice has the required access. <strong>Revise</strong>
    means a correctable content, design, link, form, metadata, or tracking issue remains.
    <strong>Blocked</strong> means a required fact, approval, account, redirect map, booking
    destination, or legal/compliance decision is missing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep the evidence with the decision: tested URLs, device and browser, screenshots when useful,
    form results, redirect checks, unresolved issues, approver, and date. This makes launch a
    controlled handoff rather than a last-minute visual opinion.
  </p>

  <h2>Compare website partners on the same questions</h2>
  <p>
    Give each provider the same current-site inventory, priority pages, booking requirements,
    access needs, and launch constraints. Then compare written scope, exclusions, decision owners,
    acceptance evidence, support, ownership, transition terms, and total commitment. A lower fee
    may exclude migration or content; a broader package may include work your practice does not
    need. The useful comparison is the complete responsibility map, not the homepage mockup alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can also use the <a href="/blog/responsive-chiropractic-website-templates/">website template
    evaluation checklist</a>, <a href="/blog/chiropractic-website-booking-conversion-audit/">booking-path
    audit</a>, and <a href="/blog/ai-built-chiropractic-website-review-checklist/">AI-built website
    review</a> to inspect the details without repeating them here.
  </p>

  <h2>Bring the current site and the unresolved questions</h2>
  <p>
    Bring your current site, provider concerns, access list, and launch priorities to a
    <a href="/schedule/">ChiroCandy discovery call</a>.
    We can identify the scope and handoff questions that need answers before a rebuild. The goal
    of the call is a clearer decision—not a promise about rankings or appointment volume.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>

